  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1217 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMuch of the previous discussion for the long term period remains\non track, with the focus being a mid-week system returning \nrain/storm chances to the region, followed by increasingly poor \nmodel agreement late in the work week reducing forecast \nconfidence. Energy from Tropical Storm Rachel (currently west of \nBaja California) will interact with a digging upper trough over \nthe western US, resulting in a deep, closed upper low over NV by \nMon night. In advance of this system, mid-level height rises \nacross TX will peak early next week, supporting above normal temps\nMon-Tues (highs in the upper 80s to mid 90s). \n\nThe gradual approach of the upper trough will lead to increasing\nSW flow and the return of rain/storm chances by Tues afternoon \n(continuing through Wed). As noted yesterday, precipitable water \n(PWAT) values will be unseasonably high, around the 95th \npercentile of climatology. Cloud layer winds around 30 kts (out of\nthe southwest) suggest appreciable forward motion for any storms,\nbut there are some indications that backbuilding elements would \nhave storm motions closer to 10 kts (more supportive of localized \nflooding potential). Given this, there is a Marginal Risk of \nExcessive Rainfall on Tues-Wed. There may also be a risk of \nlocalized, sporadic severe wind gusts as moderate instability \n(1500- 2000 J/kg), marginal wind shear (20-30 kts), and the high \nPWATs support water-loaded downdrafts.\n\nUnfortunately, guidance still diverges considerably during the\nlatter half of the work week, resulting in poor forecast \nconfidence. The ECMWF keeps the upper trough anchored over the SW \nCONUS, which would keep continued rain chances in place area-wide \nfor Thurs-Fri. The GFS quickly ejects the trough to the east, \nmerging it with another system over the upper Midwest and sending \na cold front through our FA. Relative to previous ensemble \nguidance, the probability of 1\" of rain through Fri AM has \nincreased slightly, to 20-40% on the Caprock and 40-50% off the \nCaprock, but again, forecast confidence is lower than usual for \nthis range of the forecast. The ensemble guidance also continues \nto exhibit a cooling trend after Tues, with the NBM favoring highs\nin the 60s and 70s by Fri-Sat. This range of the forecast will \nremain subject to change until model come into better agreement. \nFor what its worth, both of the deterministic, AI-based versions \nof the GFS/ECMWF, which have exhibited skill for medium-range \nsynoptic pattern verification, favor the GFS solution (faster \nevolution of the trough, lower precip chances locally beyond \nThurs, and cooler post-frontal air in place by Fri).",
